WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.202 line:0%
EVERETT BERRYMAN, JR: I have
been reluctant to tell them

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:07.573 line:0%
my story because a
lot of the youngsters

00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:09.909 line:0%
did not experience
what I experienced.

00:00:09.942 --> 00:00:12.979
They didn't go through
what I went through.

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:17.550
My experience here
was to be locked out.

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:20.520 line:0%
ABBY PHILLIP: In 1954, the
landmark Supreme Court case

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:23.189 line:0%
Brown v. Board of
Education established

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:25.391 line:0%
that racial segregation
of public schools

00:00:25.424 --> 00:00:26.893 line:0%
was unconstitutional.

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:28.928 line:0%
The case made a
little girl named

00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:31.764 line:0%
Linda Brown from
Topeka, Kansas the face

00:00:31.797 --> 00:00:33.299 line:0%
of school desegregation.

00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:35.635
But like many fights of
the Civil Rights era,

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:38.237
the story begins far
earlier than that.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.338 line:0%
CAMERON PATTERSON:
When you think

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:41.407 line:0%
of what happens here
in Prince Edward,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.043 line:0%
it's prior to the
Montgomery bus boycotts.

00:00:44.076 --> 00:00:46.512
It's prior to the
Selma-to-Montgomery

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:48.014
voting rights marches.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:50.683
And I don't think you can talk
about the modern Civil Rights

00:00:50.716 --> 00:00:54.153
movement without
understanding what happens

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:55.522
here in Prince Edward County.

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:11.104 line:0%
LARISSA SMITH: The
facilities of Black schools

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:14.941 line:0%
were generally inferior to
white schools at the time.

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.109 line:0%
ABBY PHILLIP: One
of those schools

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:19.445 line:0%
was the all-Black Robert
Russa Moton High School

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:22.448 line:0%
in Prince Edward County,
Virginia, where a museum

00:01:22.481 --> 00:01:25.351 line:0%
bearing its name now stands.

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:27.720
The school was built
for 180 students.

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:32.525
But by 1951, it was
bursting with over 450.

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:34.660
Some classes were
held in an old school

00:01:34.693 --> 00:01:39.899
bus, others in tar-paper rooms
heated with potbelly stoves.

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:42.368 line:0%
The students staged
a walkout that year,

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:45.738 line:0%
launching a two-week strike
and demanding the school board

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:48.875 line:0%
build a bigger, better school.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:50.409 line:0%
CAMERON PATTERSON:
This student walkout

00:01:50.442 --> 00:01:54.680 line:0%
would lead to a local court
case, Davis versus County

00:01:54.713 --> 00:01:56.215 line:0%
School Board at Prince Edward.

00:01:56.248 --> 00:01:59.719 line:0%
And that Davis case would
combine with cases from four

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.588 line:0%
other communities to make
up what we would know

00:02:02.621 --> 00:02:05.291 line:0%
as Brown v. Board of Education.

00:02:05.324 --> 00:02:07.426
ABBY PHILLIP: The
Brown ruling in 1954

00:02:07.459 --> 00:02:11.030
was supposed to radically alter
education for Black children.

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:13.266
But white populations
across the South

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:16.969
almost immediately fought
against its implementation,

00:02:17.002 --> 00:02:20.239
none more so than in
Prince Edward County, where

00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:22.642
that student walkout
helped propel the Brown

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.877
case to the Supreme Court.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.647 line:0%
The county closed, at
least 20 public schools

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.085 line:0%
from 1959 to 1964, denying
upwards of 1,500 students

00:02:33.118 --> 00:02:35.821 line:0%
an education for five years.

00:02:35.854 --> 00:02:39.525 line:0%
Historians regard it as one of
the fiercest acts of resistance

00:02:39.558 --> 00:02:41.327 line:0%
against school desegregation.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.763 line:0%
So what happened to the
students, the Black students

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:44.697 line:0%
and the white students?

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:46.766 line:0%
DAWN WILLIAMS: The
White Citizens' Council,

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:50.069 line:0%
within the community,
created a school,

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:52.538 line:0%
created a school that
was a private school

00:02:52.571 --> 00:02:56.776 line:0%
that opened up in Prince Edward
County just for white children.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.911
For the Black
community, there were

00:02:58.944 --> 00:03:01.180
no forms of public schooling.

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:03.883
My family, who did not
have a lot of resources,

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:06.552
were a part of that
segment of the community

00:03:06.585 --> 00:03:10.189
that did without formal
schooling for that time period.

00:03:10.222 --> 00:03:12.158
LARISSA SMITH: Civil rights
groups came into the county

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:15.161
and helped to set up
makeshift schools that

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:16.862
were called training centers.

00:03:16.895 --> 00:03:20.600 line:0%
Black women organized grassroots
schools in their homes.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:23.002 line:0%
DAWN WILLIAMS: So
imagine being a mom,

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:26.172 line:0%
and you have a school-age
child, and you send your child

00:03:26.205 --> 00:03:27.974 line:0%
away to another
family that you don't

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:32.011 line:0%
know just so that your
child can get the education.

00:03:32.044 --> 00:03:34.247
EVERETT BERRYMAN, JR: We
basically moved into the house

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.781
with the family.

00:03:35.814 --> 00:03:38.784 line:0%
The first family they
didn't have any children.

00:03:38.817 --> 00:03:43.155 line:0%
The second family had children,
but it was really a big house.

00:03:43.188 --> 00:03:46.626
They had about four children,
and five more came in.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:49.862
And we still could get lost
in the house, so to speak.

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:52.331
ABBY PHILLIP: And it didn't
only impact the students.

00:03:52.364 --> 00:03:54.533
The Black teachers
in the community

00:03:54.566 --> 00:03:57.603
lost their jobs as well.

00:03:57.636 --> 00:03:59.405 line:0%
ELLIOTT BROWN PALMER, SR: I
had to spend day and night

00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:03.042 line:0%
traveling over this state
following behind complaints

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:07.546 line:0%
of Black teachers being
dismissed where schools

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:09.415 line:0%
were being desegregated.

00:04:09.448 --> 00:04:13.419 line:0%
They tried to say, we
don't want Black teachers

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:16.022 line:0%
teaching white students.

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:17.857 line:0%
DAWN WILLIAMS: Teaching at
that time was a profession

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.625 line:0%
that was heavily lauded.

00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:21.527 line:0%
It was a profession
highly respected,

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.362 line:0%
particularly within
the Black community.

00:04:23.395 --> 00:04:26.265 line:0%
ABBY PHILLIP: In fact,
nationwide tens of thousands

00:04:26.298 --> 00:04:28.567 line:0%
of Black teachers
lost their jobs

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:33.906 line:0%
as an unanticipated consequence
of Brown v. Board of Education.

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:37.410
Taken together, the vanishing
of Black teacher jobs

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.912
and the school closings
dramatically affected

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:41.947
lives across the county.

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:45.818
One estimate found that
illiteracy rates of Blacks ages

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:52.525
5 to 22 jumped from 3% to
23% in those five years,

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:56.095
even as the push for
desegregation continued.

00:04:56.128 --> 00:04:58.964 line:0%
A pending court case
was amended in 1961

00:04:58.997 --> 00:05:03.035 line:0%
to challenge private school tax
breaks given to white families.

00:05:03.068 --> 00:05:06.839
As that case, Griffin versus
County School Board of Prince

00:05:06.872 --> 00:05:09.942
Edward County, was making
its way to the Supreme Court,

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:11.811
the Justice
Department stepped in,

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.046
encouraged by a
broader civil rights

00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:16.082
movement sweeping the South.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:21.020 line:0%
It spearheaded efforts to create
a free schools program in 1963

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:23.456 line:0%
for all children
in the community.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:25.057 line:0%
LARISSA SMITH: And they
really are interested

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.193 line:0%
in what's happening here in
Prince Edward because they

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:30.930 line:0%
understand that if a county
cannot fund its public schools

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:34.567 line:0%
and shut them down and get away
with it, then Brown would be

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.369 line:0%
a dead letter across the South.

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:38.137 line:0%
ABBY PHILLIP: And a year
later, the Supreme Court

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.706 line:0%
ruled on the Griffin
case, ordering all schools

00:05:40.739 --> 00:05:43.442 line:0%
in the county to
reopen and declaring

00:05:43.475 --> 00:05:47.246 line:0%
the time for mere deliberate
speed has run out.

00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:49.715 line:0%
When Everett R.
Berryman, Jr. eventually

00:05:49.748 --> 00:05:53.052
graduated from Moton
high school in 1967,

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:55.955
the schools were
still mostly Black.

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:57.790
EVERETT BERRYMAN, JR:
The whole experience,

00:05:57.823 --> 00:06:01.427
when I look back on it, helped
me to get to where I am today.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:05.531
Actually, it caused me
to step up to the plate

00:06:05.564 --> 00:06:08.200
and to find myself.

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:14.440
And once I found myself,
then I could go on forward.

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:16.242
ABBY PHILLIP: Today,
the public school system

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:20.880
is 65% students of color, a
hard-earned legacy of the fight

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.048
to implement the Brown mandate.

00:06:23.081 --> 00:06:27.119
But so too are the unmistakable
remnants of the racial divide.

00:06:27.152 --> 00:06:30.089
The private school that
was founded in 1959

00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:32.725
during the school
closures is still there,

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:35.528
and it remains
overwhelmingly white.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.396
And those students
of color now sit

00:06:37.429 --> 00:06:39.331
in public school classrooms.

00:06:39.364 --> 00:06:43.002
More than half of them are
economically disadvantaged.

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:46.639
One historian attributes the
poor adult literacy rates,

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:49.842
in part, to those
lost five years,

00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:54.780
when over 1,000 Black students
were denied an education.

00:06:54.813 --> 00:06:58.451
Progress, it turns out,
is complicated to define.

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:01.253
ELLIOTT BROWN PALMER, SR: Many
of us speak of the Brown era

00:07:01.286 --> 00:07:03.956
as if it's all over with.

00:07:03.989 --> 00:07:05.558
It isn't all over with.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:09.729
It's still going on,
in one form or another.

